"},{"title":"ICLR 2026 Highlights: Papers, Trends & the Review Crisis","url":"https://www.bohrium.com/en/blog/research-notes/iclr-2026-accepted-papers-highlights/"}]},"Let me try one more search specifically targeting the official review guidelines document:",{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01Auq7rXfyvBg281NXaVh5G9","content":[{"title":"ICLR 2026 Reviewer Guide","url":"https://iclr.cc/Conferences/2026/ReviewerGuide"},{"title":"A Retrospective on the ICLR 2026 Review Process – ICLR Blog","url":"https://blog.iclr.cc/2026/03/31/a-retrospective-on-the-iclr-2026-review-process/"},{"title":"Assisting ICLR 2025 reviewers with feedback – ICLR Blog","url":"https://blog.iclr.cc/2024/10/09/iclr2025-assisting-reviewers/"},{"title":"Papers","url":"https://iclr.cc/FAQ/SignatureFieldPapers"},{"title":"2026 – ICLR Blog","url":"https://blog.iclr.cc/2026/"},{"title":"ICLR 2026 Area Chair Guide","url":"https://iclr.cc/Conferences/2026/AreaChairGuide"},{"title":"Frequently Asked Questions","url":"https://iclr.cc/Conferences/2025/FAQ"},{"title":"Announcing the ICLR 2026 Outstanding Papers – ICLR Blog","url":"https://blog.iclr.cc/2026/04/23/announcing-the-iclr-2026-outstanding-papers/"},{"title":"ICLR 2022 Reviewer Instructions","url":"https://iclr.cc/Conferences/2025/ReviewerGuide"},{"title":"2026 Conference","url":"https://iclr.cc/"}]},"Let me try a more specific search to find the actual rating scale meanings:",{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01BHC37s2B168cAJFYvER8JM","content":[{"title":"The AI Review Lottery: Widespread AI-Assisted Peer Reviews Boost Paper Scores and Acceptance Rates","url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2405.02150v1"},{"title":"(PDF) Insights from the ICLR Peer Review and Rebuttal Process","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/397780200_Insights_from_the_ICLR_Peer_Review_and_Rebuttal_Process"},{"title":"Analyzing the Machine Learning Conference Review Process","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2011.12919"},{"title":"Does double-blind peer-review reduce bias? Evidence from a top computer   science conference","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2101.02701"},{"title":"An Open Review of OpenReview: A Critical Analysis of the Machine   Learning Conference Review Process","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2010.05137"},{"title":"Insights from the ICLR Peer Review and Rebuttal Process","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.15462"},{"title":"Aspect-based Sentiment Analysis of Scientific Reviews","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2006.03257"},{"title":"The AI Review Lottery: Widespread AI-Assisted Peer Reviews Boost Paper   Scores and Acceptance Rates","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2405.02150"}]},"Based on my search results, I can now provide you with information about the ICLR 2026 review form rating scale:\n\n## ICLR 2026 Review Form Rating Scale\n\nThe overall rating for ICLR 2026 is mapped to {0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10}, representing a change from previous years. The classic 1–5 scale is gone, and the new 2–4–6–8–10 system removes the middle ground, forcing reviewers into decisive opinions.\n\nHowever, while I can confirm the rating scale values used in ICLR 2026, the search results did not reveal the specific textual definitions for what each individual score (0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10) means in terms of paper quality or acceptance recommendations.\n\nFor context, I found that for earlier ICLR years (2018-2024), scores 1 and 3 indicate low-quality submissions that should typically be rejected; scores 5 and 6 indicate borderline submissions; and scores 8 and 10 indicate high-quality submissions that should typically be accepted. However, since ICLR 2026 uses a different scale with even numbers (0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10), the exact definitions for these specific score points in 2026 would require access to the official ICLR 2026 review form guidelines, which don't appear to be fully detailed in the publicly available sources I found."],"durationSeconds":16.639271524000005}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01CskPtrYVE1bQYUj6Lf3y35","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I now have enough information about ICLR 2026's review scale. The overall rating uses {0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10}. Based on general ICLR conventions and the search results:\n\n- 10: Outstanding / Top 5%\n- 8: Strong Accept / Clear accept\n- 6: Weak Accept / Marginally above threshold\n- 4: Weak Reject / Marginally below threshold\n- 2: Strong Reject / Clear reject\n- 0: Very strong reject\n\nNow I need to dispatch two reviewers in parallel:\n\n\n- R1 will focus on methods and experiments\n- R2 will focus on novelty and related work\n\nI'll launch them as agents and then conduct the roundtable discussion using the ICLR 2026 scale for scoring.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2530,"cache_read_input_tokens":61241,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2530},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7d51deee-4a05-4a6b-bde3-295058a399ba","uuid":"010791b4-9ddc-4b5f-8372-6b97b79195ed"}
